I derived the expected outputs by reducing the problem to a known structure. A robot can collect apples i then j (with T_i ≤ T_j) iff |X_j − X_i| ≤ T_j − T_i. Setting u = T+X and v = T−X turns this into 2D dominance (u and v both non-decreasing). So the answer is the minimum number of chains covering the points, which by Dilworth equals the longest strictly decreasing subsequence of v after sorting by u ascending, with ties in u sorted by v ascending.

